---
title: Bar and Rect Marks
description: Reference for barY, barX, rect, cell, interval endpoints, band sizing, grouped bars, selective corner radii, color, insets, and focus anchors.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar marks encode a numeric interval against a categorical or positional\nchannel. Rect marks encode independent x and y intervals and are the general\nprimitive for heatmaps, interval blocks, and cells."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { barX, barY, cell, group, rect, stack } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bary","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"},{"type":"text","value":" draws vertical bars from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":" at x."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const mark = barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function barY<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: BarYOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, InferredX, number>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer-derived"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable mark ID"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar category or center"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, number?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric datum"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Length; implicitly stacked at each x"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number | Channel<TDatum, number?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit stack start"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit baseline endpoint"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number | Channel<TDatum, number?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit stack end"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit value endpoint; takes precedence over y"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group identity; color fallback when omitted"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independent value sent to the chart color scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Top/nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", x, then index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable scene and interaction identity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final bar paint override"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fill opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar outline color"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar outline opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar outline width"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeDasharray"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG outline dash pattern"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GroupLayout | StackLayout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit diverging stack"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configures grouping or stack order/offset"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixels removed from both categorical edges"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxThickness"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unbounded"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maximum painted width after grouping and inset"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BarRadius<TDatum>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uniform, physical, or semantic end corner radii"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"states"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readonly ChartMarkState[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus-driven presentation overrides"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The interaction point is at the group-band center and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" endpoint.\nIts semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xValue"},{"type":"text","value":" is x and its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yValue"},{"type":"text","value":" is the value endpoint."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"barx","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"},{"type":"text","value":" draws horizontal bars from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":" at y."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const mark = barX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'category',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function barX<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: BarXOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, number, InferredY>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its options transpose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer-derived"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable mark ID"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, number?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric datum"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Length; implicitly stacked at each y"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number | Channel<TDatum, number?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit stack start"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit baseline endpoint"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number | Channel<TDatum, number?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit stack end"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit value endpoint; takes precedence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar category or center"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group identity; color fallback when omitted"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independent color-scale value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Top/nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", y, then index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable identity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final bar paint override"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fill opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GroupLayout | StackLayout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit diverging stack"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configures grouping or stack order/offset"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixels removed from both categorical edges"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxThickness"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unbounded"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maximum painted height after grouping/inset"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BarRadius<TDatum>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uniform, physical, or semantic end radii"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"states"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readonly ChartMarkState[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus-driven presentation overrides"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The interaction point is at the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" endpoint and group-band center."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bar-bandwidth","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar bandwidth"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With a band scale on the categorical axis, bars use its responsive bandwidth.\nWith a nonband scale, the mark estimates width from the smallest distance\nbetween distinct mapped positions and uses 80 percent of that distance. A\nsingle-position fallback is capped at 48 pixels."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For predictable categorical bars, use a configured band scale and set its\npadding. Scale setup and ownership are documented in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":" is applied after band or inferred layout and is clamped to at least\nzero. A sufficiently large inset produces a zero-width or zero-height bar\nrather than negative geometry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxThickness"},{"type":"text","value":" caps the painted width for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"},{"type":"text","value":" or height for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"},{"type":"text","value":" in final\nchart pixels. The mark applies the cap after grouped-band layout and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":",\nthen centers the narrower bar in its resolved band. Narrow responsive bands\nkeep their natural size. Negative finite values clamp to zero; nonfinite values\ndo not cap the bar. Inline-state "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":" overrides remain absolute, but their\nresolved geometry still honors the cap."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bar-corner-radii","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar corner radii"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a number to round every corner and preserve the existing rectangle output:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: 4,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A four-value tuple controls physical corners in top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. The order stays physical when a scale range or\nan interval is reversed. The option is a visual channel, so an accessor can\nreturn a different number or tuple for each row."}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: [6, 6, 0, 0],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" when the rounded corners should follow the semantic value endpoint.\nThis works for positive and negative values and for reversed scale ranges."}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: { end: 6 },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For an implicit stack, an omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stack"},{"type":"text","value":" rounds only the exposed positive or\nnegative stack endpoint. For explicit intervals and grouped bars, it rounds\neach bar because there is no native stack envelope. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stack: 'each'"},{"type":"text","value":" to\nround every implicit stack segment. An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stack: 'outer'"},{"type":"text","value":" requires an\nimplicit stack and rejects explicit interval endpoints and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group()"},{"type":"text","value":" layouts.\nThe outer edge comes from the resolved stack geometry. If a nondiverging\noffset makes intervals overlap at that edge, every touching interval receives\nits own radius on the same physical envelope edge so a later fill cannot square\noff the envelope. Anchor-translated stacks can expose one end on each side of\nzero, and both resolved outer ends are rounded."}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'series',\n  radius: { end: 6, stack: 'each' },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The related public types are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RectCornerRadii"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RectRadius"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BarEndRadius<TDatum>"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BarRadius<TDatum>"},{"type":"text","value":". Selective radii are clamped to\nfinite nonnegative values. When adjacent values do not fit, they shrink\nproportionally without changing their ratio. Zero-length semantic bars have\nsquare corners. Inline-state "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":" overrides accept the same number or\nphysical tuple forms."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"grouped-bars","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouped bars"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping is explicit:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'region',\n  layout: group(),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a secondary band scale inside the primary categorical band.\nUse "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group({ padding: 0.2 })"},{"type":"text","value":" for the common spacing control, or pass\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group({ scale })"},{"type":"text","value":" when subgroup order is fixed application state.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GroupOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" is the reusable configuration shape for those "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"padding"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale"},{"type":"text","value":" controls."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies subgroup identity. If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted, a discrete\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel may supply identity after grouped geometry has been selected.\nWhen both are present, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" controls placement and interaction grouping while\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" remains independent. A continuous color channel cannot infer series."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark throws when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the scale has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandwidth"},{"type":"text","value":" method"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a grouped row has a null effective group value"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a group value is outside the group-scale domain or maps to a nonfinite position"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repeated positions stack by default. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout: group()"},{"type":"text","value":" is the explicit opt-in\nto side-by-side geometry."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"stacked-bars","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stacked bars"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The single value channel is a length. Repeated categorical positions stack\nautomatically, with positive and negative values diverging from zero:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'region',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" when series identity differs from color. Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout: stack()"},{"type":"text","value":" only\nwhen the default stack needs a configured order or offset:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'segment',\n  layout: stack({\n    order: ['Core', 'Services'],\n    offset: 'normalize',\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"order"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts input order, ascending or descending absolute totals,\ninside-out streamgraph order, or an explicit series list. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"},{"type":"text","value":" reverses\nthe resolved order. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"diverging"},{"type":"text","value":" (default), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"center"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wiggle"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"type StackOrder =\n  'input' | 'ascending' | 'descending' | 'inside-out' | readonly ChartKey[]\ntype StackOffset = 'diverging' | 'normalize' | 'center' | 'wiggle'\n\ninterface StackAnchor {\n  series: ChartKey\n  fraction?: number\n}\n\ninterface StackOptions {\n  order?: StackOrder\n  offset?: StackOffset\n  reverse?: boolean\n  anchor?: StackAnchor\n}\n\ninterface StackLayout extends StackOptions {\n  readonly type: 'stack'\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inside-out"},{"type":"text","value":" uses each series peak and total to balance layers above and below\nthe stream. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wiggle"},{"type":"text","value":" is intended for nonnegative streamgraph values. After the\nwiggle offset is complete, Charts translates the whole stack so its global\nminimum start is zero. It does not independently rebase each position."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor"},{"type":"text","value":" when ordered, nonnegative category counts should diverge around a\npoint inside one series. This Likert layout places the midpoint of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Neutral"},{"type":"text","value":" at\nzero:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barX(counts, {\n  x: 'count',\n  y: 'question',\n  z: 'response',\n  color: 'response',\n  layout: stack({\n    order: ['Strongly Disagree', 'Disagree', 'Neutral', 'Agree'],\n    anchor: { series: 'Neutral', fraction: 0.5 },\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fraction"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.5"},{"type":"text","value":"; zero selects the series start and one selects its\nend. Anchor layout zero-imputes missing position/series cells before translating\nthe completed stack, but it emits no synthetic rows. The anchor series may be\nabsent at a position. It must still appear in the resolved series order, which\nan explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"order"},{"type":"text","value":" can establish. Anchors reject negative lengths and cannot\nbe combined with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"center"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wiggle"},{"type":"text","value":" offsets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positions and series retain first-seen order. Missing position/series pairs\ncontribute zero to layout without creating synthetic rows or interaction\npoints."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supplying "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":" opts out of implicit stacking and treats the channels\nas authored endpoints. The same contract is transposed for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bar-baselines-and-invalid-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar baselines and invalid rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implicit stacks include zero in inferred quantitative domains. Explicit\nendpoints contribute their authored bounds. The configured scale still owns\nits semantic domain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rows are skipped when their category, baseline, or endpoint is invalid.\nNegative and reversed intervals are supported because geometry uses the\nminimum mapped endpoint and absolute length."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bandx-and-bandy","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandY"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandX"},{"type":"text","value":" paints the complete plot height at each x value. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandY"},{"type":"text","value":" paints the\ncomplete plot width at each y value. They are ordinary marks used for\ncategorical backgrounds and focus presentation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"whenFocused(\n  bandX(rows, {\n    x: 'category',\n    fill: '#64748b',\n    fillOpacity: 0.14,\n    inset: -6,\n  }),\n  { match: 'x' },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BandXOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BandYOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" provide the positional channel, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandX.width"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandY.height"},{"type":"text","value":" can replace scale or inferred bandwidth with an explicit\nnonnegative scene-pixel size, which is useful for a one-pixel focus cursor. A\nnegative inset expands the resolved band. The helpers are also available from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/band"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rect","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":" draws one independent x/y interval per valid row:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const mark = rect(events, {\n  x1: 'start',\n  x2: 'end',\n  y: 'lane',\n  z: 'status',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function rect<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: RectOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<\n  TDatum,\n  InferredPointX,\n  InferredPointY,\n  InferredScaleX,\n  InferredScaleY\n>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"options-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer-derived"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable mark ID"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"X center/category and preferred semantic focus value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", or row index when x is absent"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First x endpoint"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second x endpoint"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric datum"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Y center/category and preferred semantic focus value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First y endpoint"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second y endpoint"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction group"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value sent to the chart color scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Top/nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", x/y tuple, then index"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable identity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final constant fill override"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fill opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Constant stroke"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stroke width"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.75"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixels removed from all four edges"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, RectRadius>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uniform or physical per-corner radii"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"states"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readonly ChartMarkState[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus-driven presentation overrides"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both endpoints must be valid chart values. Endpoint order may be reversed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rect and cell corner tuples always use physical top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. Reversing either semantic endpoint does not\nreorder the tuple."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When two semantic endpoints are equal and the resolved scale has bandwidth,\nthe rect spans that complete band. Otherwise it spans the mapped endpoint\ndistance. This lets "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x: 'column', y: 'row'"},{"type":"text","value":" create a heatmap cell without\nmanually deriving boundaries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The interaction coordinate is the geometric center before inset. Semantic\npoint values use a valid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":". An omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to the row index;\nan invalid x falls back to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":". An omitted or invalid y falls back to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":"\nunless the datum itself is numeric. Scale typing still includes all interval\nendpoints, so a heterogeneous interval remains honest without widening\ninteraction callbacks unnecessarily."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":" without explicit endpoint options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const mark = cell(rows, {\n  x: 'weekday',\n  y: 'week',\n  z: 'bucket',\n  fillOpacity: 0.9,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"type CellOptions<TDatum> = Omit<RectOptions<TDatum>, 'x1' | 'x2' | 'y1' | 'y2'>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both axes normally use band scales. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" shares rect rendering, defaults,\nfocus behavior, and class names."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"bary","text":"barY","level":2},{"id":"options","text":"Options","level":3},{"id":"barx","text":"barX","level":2},{"id":"bar-bandwidth","text":"Bar bandwidth","level":2},{"id":"bar-corner-radii","text":"Bar corner radii","level":2},{"id":"grouped-bars","text":"Grouped bars","level":2},{"id":"stacked-bars","text":"Stacked bars","level":2},{"id":"bar-baselines-and-invalid-rows","text":"Bar baselines and invalid rows","level":2},{"id":"bandx-and-bandy","text":"bandX and bandY","level":2},{"id":"rect","text":"rect","level":2},{"id":"options-2","text":"Options","level":3},{"id":"cell","text":"cell","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nBar marks encode a numeric interval against a categorical or positional\nchannel. Rect marks encode independent x and y intervals and are the general\nprimitive for heatmaps, interval blocks, and cells.\n\n```ts\nimport { barX, barY, cell, group, rect, stack } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n```\n\n## `barY`\n\n`barY` draws vertical bars from `y1` to `y2` at x.\n\n```ts\nconst mark = barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction barY<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: BarYOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, InferredX, number>\n```\n\n### Options\n\n| Option            | Type                                 | Default                        | Meaning                                          |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `id`              | `string`                             | Layer-derived                  | Stable mark ID                                   |\n| `x`               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`       | Row index                      | Bar category or center                           |\n| `y`               | `Channel<TDatum, number?>`           | Numeric datum                  | Length; implicitly stacked at each x             |\n| `y1`              | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack start           | Explicit baseline endpoint                       |\n| `y2`              | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack end             | Explicit value endpoint; takes precedence over y |\n| `z`               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | No group                       | Group identity; color fallback when omitted      |\n| `color`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | `z`                            | Independent value sent to the chart color scale  |\n| `key`             | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`          | Top/nested `id`, x, then index | Stable scene and interaction identity            |\n| `fill`            | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | Resolved `color`               | Final bar paint override                         |\n| `fillOpacity`     | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Fill opacity                                     |\n| `stroke`          | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | None                           | Bar outline color                                |\n| `strokeOpacity`   | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Bar outline opacity                              |\n| `strokeWidth`     | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Bar outline width                                |\n| `strokeDasharray` | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | None                           | SVG outline dash pattern                         |\n| `layout`          | `GroupLayout \\| StackLayout`         | Implicit diverging stack       | Configures grouping or stack order/offset        |\n| `inset`           | `number`                             | `0`                            | Pixels removed from both categorical edges       |\n| `maxThickness`    | `number`                             | Unbounded                      | Maximum painted width after grouping and inset   |\n| `radius`          | `BarRadius<TDatum>`                  | None                           | Uniform, physical, or semantic end corner radii  |\n| `states`          | `readonly ChartMarkState[]`          | None                           | Focus-driven presentation overrides              |\n\nThe interaction point is at the group-band center and the `y2`/`y` endpoint.\nIts semantic `xValue` is x and its `yValue` is the value endpoint.\n\n## `barX`\n\n`barX` draws horizontal bars from `x1` to `x2` at y.\n\n```ts\nconst mark = barX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'category',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction barX<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: BarXOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, number, InferredY>\n```\n\nIts options transpose `barY`:\n\n| Option         | Type                                 | Default                        | Meaning                                     |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`           | `string`                             | Layer-derived                  | Stable mark ID                              |\n| `x`            | `Channel<TDatum, number?>`           | Numeric datum                  | Length; implicitly stacked at each y        |\n| `x1`           | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack start           | Explicit baseline endpoint                  |\n| `x2`           | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack end             | Explicit value endpoint; takes precedence   |\n| `y`            | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`       | Row index                      | Bar category or center                      |\n| `z`            | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | No group                       | Group identity; color fallback when omitted |\n| `color`        | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | `z`                            | Independent color-scale value               |\n| `key`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`          | Top/nested `id`, y, then index | Stable identity                             |\n| `fill`         | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | Resolved `color`               | Final bar paint override                    |\n| `fillOpacity`  | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Fill opacity                                |\n| `layout`       | `GroupLayout \\| StackLayout`         | Implicit diverging stack       | Configures grouping or stack order/offset   |\n| `inset`        | `number`                             | `0`                            | Pixels removed from both categorical edges  |\n| `maxThickness` | `number`                             | Unbounded                      | Maximum painted height after grouping/inset |\n| `radius`       | `BarRadius<TDatum>`                  | None                           | Uniform, physical, or semantic end radii    |\n| `states`       | `readonly ChartMarkState[]`          | None                           | Focus-driven presentation overrides         |\n\nThe interaction point is at the `x2`/`x` endpoint and group-band center.\n\n## Bar bandwidth\n\nWith a band scale on the categorical axis, bars use its responsive bandwidth.\nWith a nonband scale, the mark estimates width from the smallest distance\nbetween distinct mapped positions and uses 80 percent of that distance. A\nsingle-position fallback is capped at 48 pixels.\n\nFor predictable categorical bars, use a configured band scale and set its\npadding. Scale setup and ownership are documented in\n[Scales](../../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n`inset` is applied after band or inferred layout and is clamped to at least\nzero. A sufficiently large inset produces a zero-width or zero-height bar\nrather than negative geometry.\n\n`maxThickness` caps the painted width for `barY` or height for `barX` in final\nchart pixels. The mark applies the cap after grouped-band layout and `inset`,\nthen centers the narrower bar in its resolved band. Narrow responsive bands\nkeep their natural size. Negative finite values clamp to zero; nonfinite values\ndo not cap the bar. Inline-state `inset` overrides remain absolute, but their\nresolved geometry still honors the cap.\n\n## Bar corner radii\n\nUse a number to round every corner and preserve the existing rectangle output:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: 4,\n})\n```\n\nA four-value tuple controls physical corners in top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. The order stays physical when a scale range or\nan interval is reversed. The option is a visual channel, so an accessor can\nreturn a different number or tuple for each row.\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: [6, 6, 0, 0],\n})\n```\n\nUse `end` when the rounded corners should follow the semantic value endpoint.\nThis works for positive and negative values and for reversed scale ranges.\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: { end: 6 },\n})\n```\n\nFor an implicit stack, an omitted `stack` rounds only the exposed positive or\nnegative stack endpoint. For explicit intervals and grouped bars, it rounds\neach bar because there is no native stack envelope. Set `stack: 'each'` to\nround every implicit stack segment. An explicit `stack: 'outer'` requires an\nimplicit stack and rejects explicit interval endpoints and `group()` layouts.\nThe outer edge comes from the resolved stack geometry. If a nondiverging\noffset makes intervals overlap at that edge, every touching interval receives\nits own radius on the same physical envelope edge so a later fill cannot square\noff the envelope. Anchor-translated stacks can expose one end on each side of\nzero, and both resolved outer ends are rounded.\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'series',\n  radius: { end: 6, stack: 'each' },\n})\n```\n\nThe related public types are `RectCornerRadii`, `RectRadius`,\n`BarEndRadius<TDatum>`, and `BarRadius<TDatum>`. Selective radii are clamped to\nfinite nonnegative values. When adjacent values do not fit, they shrink\nproportionally without changing their ratio. Zero-length semantic bars have\nsquare corners. Inline-state `radius` overrides accept the same number or\nphysical tuple forms.\n\n## Grouped bars\n\nGrouping is explicit:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'region',\n  layout: group(),\n})\n```\n\n`group()` creates a secondary band scale inside the primary categorical band.\nUse `group({ padding: 0.2 })` for the common spacing control, or pass\n`group({ scale })` when subgroup order is fixed application state.\n`GroupOptions` is the reusable configuration shape for those `padding` and\n`scale` controls.\n\nAn explicit `z` supplies subgroup identity. If `z` is omitted, a discrete\n`color` channel may supply identity after grouped geometry has been selected.\nWhen both are present, `z` controls placement and interaction grouping while\n`color` remains independent. A continuous color channel cannot infer series.\n\nThe mark throws when:\n\n- the scale has no `bandwidth` method\n- a grouped row has a null effective group value\n- a group value is outside the group-scale domain or maps to a nonfinite position\n\nRepeated positions stack by default. `layout: group()` is the explicit opt-in\nto side-by-side geometry.\n\n## Stacked bars\n\nThe single value channel is a length. Repeated categorical positions stack\nautomatically, with positive and negative values diverging from zero:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nUse `z` when series identity differs from color. Add `layout: stack()` only\nwhen the default stack needs a configured order or offset:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'segment',\n  layout: stack({\n    order: ['Core', 'Services'],\n    offset: 'normalize',\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n`order` accepts input order, ascending or descending absolute totals,\ninside-out streamgraph order, or an explicit series list. `reverse` reverses\nthe resolved order. `offset` accepts `diverging` (default), `normalize`,\n`center`, or `wiggle`.\n\n```ts\ntype StackOrder =\n  'input' | 'ascending' | 'descending' | 'inside-out' | readonly ChartKey[]\ntype StackOffset = 'diverging' | 'normalize' | 'center' | 'wiggle'\n\ninterface StackAnchor {\n  series: ChartKey\n  fraction?: number\n}\n\ninterface StackOptions {\n  order?: StackOrder\n  offset?: StackOffset\n  reverse?: boolean\n  anchor?: StackAnchor\n}\n\ninterface StackLayout extends StackOptions {\n  readonly type: 'stack'\n}\n```\n\n`inside-out` uses each series peak and total to balance layers above and below\nthe stream. `wiggle` is intended for nonnegative streamgraph values. After the\nwiggle offset is complete, Charts translates the whole stack so its global\nminimum start is zero. It does not independently rebase each position.\n\nUse `anchor` when ordered, nonnegative category counts should diverge around a\npoint inside one series. This Likert layout places the midpoint of `Neutral` at\nzero:\n\n```ts\nbarX(counts, {\n  x: 'count',\n  y: 'question',\n  z: 'response',\n  color: 'response',\n  layout: stack({\n    order: ['Strongly Disagree', 'Disagree', 'Neutral', 'Agree'],\n    anchor: { series: 'Neutral', fraction: 0.5 },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n`fraction` defaults to `0.5`; zero selects the series start and one selects its\nend. Anchor layout zero-imputes missing position/series cells before translating\nthe completed stack, but it emits no synthetic rows. The anchor series may be\nabsent at a position. It must still appear in the resolved series order, which\nan explicit `order` can establish. Anchors reject negative lengths and cannot\nbe combined with `normalize`, `center`, or `wiggle` offsets.\n\nPositions and series retain first-seen order. Missing position/series pairs\ncontribute zero to layout without creating synthetic rows or interaction\npoints.\n\nSupplying `y1` or `y2` opts out of implicit stacking and treats the channels\nas authored endpoints. The same contract is transposed for `barX`.\n\n## Bar baselines and invalid rows\n\nImplicit stacks include zero in inferred quantitative domains. Explicit\nendpoints contribute their authored bounds. The configured scale still owns\nits semantic domain.\n\nRows are skipped when their category, baseline, or endpoint is invalid.\nNegative and reversed intervals are supported because geometry uses the\nminimum mapped endpoint and absolute length.\n\n## `bandX` and `bandY`\n\n`bandX` paints the complete plot height at each x value. `bandY` paints the\ncomplete plot width at each y value. They are ordinary marks used for\ncategorical backgrounds and focus presentation:\n\n```ts\nwhenFocused(\n  bandX(rows, {\n    x: 'category',\n    fill: '#64748b',\n    fillOpacity: 0.14,\n    inset: -6,\n  }),\n  { match: 'x' },\n)\n```\n\n`BandXOptions` and `BandYOptions` provide the positional channel, `z`, `color`,\n`key`, `fill`, `fillOpacity`, `inset`, and `radius`. `bandX.width` and\n`bandY.height` can replace scale or inferred bandwidth with an explicit\nnonnegative scene-pixel size, which is useful for a one-pixel focus cursor. A\nnegative inset expands the resolved band. The helpers are also available from\n`@tanstack/charts/band`.\n\n## `rect`\n\n`rect` draws one independent x/y interval per valid row:\n\n```ts\nconst mark = rect(events, {\n  x1: 'start',\n  x2: 'end',\n  y: 'lane',\n  z: 'status',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction rect<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: RectOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<\n  TDatum,\n  InferredPointX,\n  InferredPointY,\n  InferredScaleX,\n  InferredScaleY\n>\n```\n\n### Options\n\n| Option        | Type                                | Default                                | Meaning                                              |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`          | `string`                            | Layer-derived                          | Stable mark ID                                       |\n| `x`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | Row index                              | X center/category and preferred semantic focus value |\n| `x1`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `x`, or row index when x is absent     | First x endpoint                                     |\n| `x2`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `x`                                    | Second x endpoint                                    |\n| `y`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | Numeric datum                          | Y center/category and preferred semantic focus value |\n| `y1`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `y`                                    | First y endpoint                                     |\n| `y2`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `y`                                    | Second y endpoint                                    |\n| `z`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`        | No group                               | Interaction group                                    |\n| `color`       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`        | `z`                                    | Value sent to the chart color scale                  |\n| `key`         | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`         | Top/nested `id`, x/y tuple, then index | Stable identity                                      |\n| `fill`        | `string`                            | Resolved color                         | Final constant fill override                         |\n| `fillOpacity` | `number`                            | SVG default                            | Fill opacity                                         |\n| `stroke`      | `string`                            | None                                   | Constant stroke                                      |\n| `strokeWidth` | `number`                            | SVG default                            | Stroke width                                         |\n| `inset`       | `number`                            | `0.75`                                 | Pixels removed from all four edges                   |\n| `radius`      | `VisualChannel<TDatum, RectRadius>` | None                                   | Uniform or physical per-corner radii                 |\n| `states`      | `readonly ChartMarkState[]`         | None                                   | Focus-driven presentation overrides                  |\n\nBoth endpoints must be valid chart values. Endpoint order may be reversed.\n\nRect and cell corner tuples always use physical top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. Reversing either semantic endpoint does not\nreorder the tuple.\n\nWhen two semantic endpoints are equal and the resolved scale has bandwidth,\nthe rect spans that complete band. Otherwise it spans the mapped endpoint\ndistance. This lets `x: 'column', y: 'row'` create a heatmap cell without\nmanually deriving boundaries.\n\nThe interaction coordinate is the geometric center before inset. Semantic\npoint values use a valid `x` and `y`. An omitted `x` defaults to the row index;\nan invalid x falls back to `x2`. An omitted or invalid y falls back to `y2`\nunless the datum itself is numeric. Scale typing still includes all interval\nendpoints, so a heterogeneous interval remains honest without widening\ninteraction callbacks unnecessarily.\n\n## `cell`\n\n`cell` is `rect` without explicit endpoint options:\n\n```ts\nconst mark = cell(rows, {\n  x: 'weekday',\n  y: 'week',\n  z: 'bucket',\n  fillOpacity: 0.9,\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ntype CellOptions<TDatum> = Omit<RectOptions<TDatum>, 'x1' | 'x2' | 'y1' | 'y2'>\n```\n\nBoth axes normally use band scales. `cell` shares rect rendering, defaults,\nfocus behavior, and class names.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Bar and Rect Marks\ndescription: Reference for barY, barX, rect, cell, interval endpoints, band sizing, grouped bars, selective corner radii, color, insets, and focus anchors.\n---\n\nBar marks encode a numeric interval against a categorical or positional\nchannel. Rect marks encode independent x and y intervals and are the general\nprimitive for heatmaps, interval blocks, and cells.\n\n```ts\nimport { barX, barY, cell, group, rect, stack } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n```\n\n## `barY`\n\n`barY` draws vertical bars from `y1` to `y2` at x.\n\n```ts\nconst mark = barY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction barY<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: BarYOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, InferredX, number>\n```\n\n### Options\n\n| Option            | Type                                 | Default                        | Meaning                                          |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `id`              | `string`                             | Layer-derived                  | Stable mark ID                                   |\n| `x`               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`       | Row index                      | Bar category or center                           |\n| `y`               | `Channel<TDatum, number?>`           | Numeric datum                  | Length; implicitly stacked at each x             |\n| `y1`              | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack start           | Explicit baseline endpoint                       |\n| `y2`              | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack end             | Explicit value endpoint; takes precedence over y |\n| `z`               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | No group                       | Group identity; color fallback when omitted      |\n| `color`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | `z`                            | Independent value sent to the chart color scale  |\n| `key`             | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`          | Top/nested `id`, x, then index | Stable scene and interaction identity            |\n| `fill`            | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | Resolved `color`               | Final bar paint override                         |\n| `fillOpacity`     | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Fill opacity                                     |\n| `stroke`          | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | None                           | Bar outline color                                |\n| `strokeOpacity`   | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Bar outline opacity                              |\n| `strokeWidth`     | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Bar outline width                                |\n| `strokeDasharray` | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | None                           | SVG outline dash pattern                         |\n| `layout`          | `GroupLayout \\| StackLayout`         | Implicit diverging stack       | Configures grouping or stack order/offset        |\n| `inset`           | `number`                             | `0`                            | Pixels removed from both categorical edges       |\n| `maxThickness`    | `number`                             | Unbounded                      | Maximum painted width after grouping and inset   |\n| `radius`          | `BarRadius<TDatum>`                  | None                           | Uniform, physical, or semantic end corner radii  |\n| `states`          | `readonly ChartMarkState[]`          | None                           | Focus-driven presentation overrides              |\n\nThe interaction point is at the group-band center and the `y2`/`y` endpoint.\nIts semantic `xValue` is x and its `yValue` is the value endpoint.\n\n## `barX`\n\n`barX` draws horizontal bars from `x1` to `x2` at y.\n\n```ts\nconst mark = barX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'category',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction barX<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options?: BarXOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, number, InferredY>\n```\n\nIts options transpose `barY`:\n\n| Option         | Type                                 | Default                        | Meaning                                     |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`           | `string`                             | Layer-derived                  | Stable mark ID                              |\n| `x`            | `Channel<TDatum, number?>`           | Numeric datum                  | Length; implicitly stacked at each y        |\n| `x1`           | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack start           | Explicit baseline endpoint                  |\n| `x2`           | `number \\| Channel<TDatum, number?>` | Implicit stack end             | Explicit value endpoint; takes precedence   |\n| `y`            | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`       | Row index                      | Bar category or center                      |\n| `z`            | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | No group                       | Group identity; color fallback when omitted |\n| `color`        | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`         | `z`                            | Independent color-scale value               |\n| `key`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`          | Top/nested `id`, y, then index | Stable identity                             |\n| `fill`         | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`      | Resolved `color`               | Final bar paint override                    |\n| `fillOpacity`  | `number`                             | SVG default                    | Fill opacity                                |\n| `layout`       | `GroupLayout \\| StackLayout`         | Implicit diverging stack       | Configures grouping or stack order/offset   |\n| `inset`        | `number`                             | `0`                            | Pixels removed from both categorical edges  |\n| `maxThickness` | `number`                             | Unbounded                      | Maximum painted height after grouping/inset |\n| `radius`       | `BarRadius<TDatum>`                  | None                           | Uniform, physical, or semantic end radii    |\n| `states`       | `readonly ChartMarkState[]`          | None                           | Focus-driven presentation overrides         |\n\nThe interaction point is at the `x2`/`x` endpoint and group-band center.\n\n## Bar bandwidth\n\nWith a band scale on the categorical axis, bars use its responsive bandwidth.\nWith a nonband scale, the mark estimates width from the smallest distance\nbetween distinct mapped positions and uses 80 percent of that distance. A\nsingle-position fallback is capped at 48 pixels.\n\nFor predictable categorical bars, use a configured band scale and set its\npadding. Scale setup and ownership are documented in\n[Scales](../../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n`inset` is applied after band or inferred layout and is clamped to at least\nzero. A sufficiently large inset produces a zero-width or zero-height bar\nrather than negative geometry.\n\n`maxThickness` caps the painted width for `barY` or height for `barX` in final\nchart pixels. The mark applies the cap after grouped-band layout and `inset`,\nthen centers the narrower bar in its resolved band. Narrow responsive bands\nkeep their natural size. Negative finite values clamp to zero; nonfinite values\ndo not cap the bar. Inline-state `inset` overrides remain absolute, but their\nresolved geometry still honors the cap.\n\n## Bar corner radii\n\nUse a number to round every corner and preserve the existing rectangle output:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: 4,\n})\n```\n\nA four-value tuple controls physical corners in top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. The order stays physical when a scale range or\nan interval is reversed. The option is a visual channel, so an accessor can\nreturn a different number or tuple for each row.\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: [6, 6, 0, 0],\n})\n```\n\nUse `end` when the rounded corners should follow the semantic value endpoint.\nThis works for positive and negative values and for reversed scale ranges.\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  radius: { end: 6 },\n})\n```\n\nFor an implicit stack, an omitted `stack` rounds only the exposed positive or\nnegative stack endpoint. For explicit intervals and grouped bars, it rounds\neach bar because there is no native stack envelope. Set `stack: 'each'` to\nround every implicit stack segment. An explicit `stack: 'outer'` requires an\nimplicit stack and rejects explicit interval endpoints and `group()` layouts.\nThe outer edge comes from the resolved stack geometry. If a nondiverging\noffset makes intervals overlap at that edge, every touching interval receives\nits own radius on the same physical envelope edge so a later fill cannot square\noff the envelope. Anchor-translated stacks can expose one end on each side of\nzero, and both resolved outer ends are rounded.\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'series',\n  radius: { end: 6, stack: 'each' },\n})\n```\n\nThe related public types are `RectCornerRadii`, `RectRadius`,\n`BarEndRadius<TDatum>`, and `BarRadius<TDatum>`. Selective radii are clamped to\nfinite nonnegative values. When adjacent values do not fit, they shrink\nproportionally without changing their ratio. Zero-length semantic bars have\nsquare corners. Inline-state `radius` overrides accept the same number or\nphysical tuple forms.\n\n## Grouped bars\n\nGrouping is explicit:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'region',\n  layout: group(),\n})\n```\n\n`group()` creates a secondary band scale inside the primary categorical band.\nUse `group({ padding: 0.2 })` for the common spacing control, or pass\n`group({ scale })` when subgroup order is fixed application state.\n`GroupOptions` is the reusable configuration shape for those `padding` and\n`scale` controls.\n\nAn explicit `z` supplies subgroup identity. If `z` is omitted, a discrete\n`color` channel may supply identity after grouped geometry has been selected.\nWhen both are present, `z` controls placement and interaction grouping while\n`color` remains independent. A continuous color channel cannot infer series.\n\nThe mark throws when:\n\n- the scale has no `bandwidth` method\n- a grouped row has a null effective group value\n- a group value is outside the group-scale domain or maps to a nonfinite position\n\nRepeated positions stack by default. `layout: group()` is the explicit opt-in\nto side-by-side geometry.\n\n## Stacked bars\n\nThe single value channel is a length. Repeated categorical positions stack\nautomatically, with positive and negative values diverging from zero:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nUse `z` when series identity differs from color. Add `layout: stack()` only\nwhen the default stack needs a configured order or offset:\n\n```ts\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'quarter',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  color: 'segment',\n  layout: stack({\n    order: ['Core', 'Services'],\n    offset: 'normalize',\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n`order` accepts input order, ascending or descending absolute totals,\ninside-out streamgraph order, or an explicit series list. `reverse` reverses\nthe resolved order. `offset` accepts `diverging` (default), `normalize`,\n`center`, or `wiggle`.\n\n```ts\ntype StackOrder =\n  'input' | 'ascending' | 'descending' | 'inside-out' | readonly ChartKey[]\ntype StackOffset = 'diverging' | 'normalize' | 'center' | 'wiggle'\n\ninterface StackAnchor {\n  series: ChartKey\n  fraction?: number\n}\n\ninterface StackOptions {\n  order?: StackOrder\n  offset?: StackOffset\n  reverse?: boolean\n  anchor?: StackAnchor\n}\n\ninterface StackLayout extends StackOptions {\n  readonly type: 'stack'\n}\n```\n\n`inside-out` uses each series peak and total to balance layers above and below\nthe stream. `wiggle` is intended for nonnegative streamgraph values. After the\nwiggle offset is complete, Charts translates the whole stack so its global\nminimum start is zero. It does not independently rebase each position.\n\nUse `anchor` when ordered, nonnegative category counts should diverge around a\npoint inside one series. This Likert layout places the midpoint of `Neutral` at\nzero:\n\n```ts\nbarX(counts, {\n  x: 'count',\n  y: 'question',\n  z: 'response',\n  color: 'response',\n  layout: stack({\n    order: ['Strongly Disagree', 'Disagree', 'Neutral', 'Agree'],\n    anchor: { series: 'Neutral', fraction: 0.5 },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n`fraction` defaults to `0.5`; zero selects the series start and one selects its\nend. Anchor layout zero-imputes missing position/series cells before translating\nthe completed stack, but it emits no synthetic rows. The anchor series may be\nabsent at a position. It must still appear in the resolved series order, which\nan explicit `order` can establish. Anchors reject negative lengths and cannot\nbe combined with `normalize`, `center`, or `wiggle` offsets.\n\nPositions and series retain first-seen order. Missing position/series pairs\ncontribute zero to layout without creating synthetic rows or interaction\npoints.\n\nSupplying `y1` or `y2` opts out of implicit stacking and treats the channels\nas authored endpoints. The same contract is transposed for `barX`.\n\n## Bar baselines and invalid rows\n\nImplicit stacks include zero in inferred quantitative domains. Explicit\nendpoints contribute their authored bounds. The configured scale still owns\nits semantic domain.\n\nRows are skipped when their category, baseline, or endpoint is invalid.\nNegative and reversed intervals are supported because geometry uses the\nminimum mapped endpoint and absolute length.\n\n## `bandX` and `bandY`\n\n`bandX` paints the complete plot height at each x value. `bandY` paints the\ncomplete plot width at each y value. They are ordinary marks used for\ncategorical backgrounds and focus presentation:\n\n```ts\nwhenFocused(\n  bandX(rows, {\n    x: 'category',\n    fill: '#64748b',\n    fillOpacity: 0.14,\n    inset: -6,\n  }),\n  { match: 'x' },\n)\n```\n\n`BandXOptions` and `BandYOptions` provide the positional channel, `z`, `color`,\n`key`, `fill`, `fillOpacity`, `inset`, and `radius`. `bandX.width` and\n`bandY.height` can replace scale or inferred bandwidth with an explicit\nnonnegative scene-pixel size, which is useful for a one-pixel focus cursor. A\nnegative inset expands the resolved band. The helpers are also available from\n`@tanstack/charts/band`.\n\n## `rect`\n\n`rect` draws one independent x/y interval per valid row:\n\n```ts\nconst mark = rect(events, {\n  x1: 'start',\n  x2: 'end',\n  y: 'lane',\n  z: 'status',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction rect<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: RectOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<\n  TDatum,\n  InferredPointX,\n  InferredPointY,\n  InferredScaleX,\n  InferredScaleY\n>\n```\n\n### Options\n\n| Option        | Type                                | Default                                | Meaning                                              |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`          | `string`                            | Layer-derived                          | Stable mark ID                                       |\n| `x`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | Row index                              | X center/category and preferred semantic focus value |\n| `x1`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `x`, or row index when x is absent     | First x endpoint                                     |\n| `x2`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `x`                                    | Second x endpoint                                    |\n| `y`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | Numeric datum                          | Y center/category and preferred semantic focus value |\n| `y1`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `y`                                    | First y endpoint                                     |\n| `y2`          | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`      | `y`                                    | Second y endpoint                                    |\n| `z`           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`        | No group                               | Interaction group                                    |\n| `color`       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`        | `z`                                    | Value sent to the chart color scale                  |\n| `key`         | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`         | Top/nested `id`, x/y tuple, then index | Stable identity                                      |\n| `fill`        | `string`                            | Resolved color                         | Final constant fill override                         |\n| `fillOpacity` | `number`                            | SVG default                            | Fill opacity                                         |\n| `stroke`      | `string`                            | None                                   | Constant stroke                                      |\n| `strokeWidth` | `number`                            | SVG default                            | Stroke width                                         |\n| `inset`       | `number`                            | `0.75`                                 | Pixels removed from all four edges                   |\n| `radius`      | `VisualChannel<TDatum, RectRadius>` | None                                   | Uniform or physical per-corner radii                 |\n| `states`      | `readonly ChartMarkState[]`         | None                                   | Focus-driven presentation overrides                  |\n\nBoth endpoints must be valid chart values. Endpoint order may be reversed.\n\nRect and cell corner tuples always use physical top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. Reversing either semantic endpoint does not\nreorder the tuple.\n\nWhen two semantic endpoints are equal and the resolved scale has bandwidth,\nthe rect spans that complete band. Otherwise it spans the mapped endpoint\ndistance. This lets `x: 'column', y: 'row'` create a heatmap cell without\nmanually deriving boundaries.\n\nThe interaction coordinate is the geometric center before inset. Semantic\npoint values use a valid `x` and `y`. An omitted `x` defaults to the row index;\nan invalid x falls back to `x2`. An omitted or invalid y falls back to `y2`\nunless the datum itself is numeric. Scale typing still includes all interval\nendpoints, so a heterogeneous interval remains honest without widening\ninteraction callbacks unnecessarily.\n\n## `cell`\n\n`cell` is `rect` without explicit endpoint options:\n\n```ts\nconst mark = cell(rows, {\n  x: 'weekday',\n  y: 'week',\n  z: 'bucket',\n  fillOpacity: 0.9,\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ntype CellOptions<TDatum> = Omit<RectOptions<TDatum>, 'x1' | 'x2' | 'y1' | 'y2'>\n```\n\nBoth axes normally use band scales. `cell` shares rect rendering, defaults,\nfocus behavior, and class names.\n"}
